      <div id="itch-factor" class="mt-20 bg-[#1E90FF]/5 rounded-3xl p-5 sm:p-8 border border-[#1E90FF]/20 scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">The Itch Factor: How to Prevent Post-Shave Regrowth Itch</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 space-y-4">
          <p>
            The #1 reason men stop shaving their back is the "third-day itch." When thousands of short, blunt hairs begin to emerge from the follicle at once, they can irritate the surrounding skin, leading to intense itching and even "shaving bumps."
          </p>
          <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mt-6">
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why It Happens</h4>
              <p class="text-sm">Regrowth itch is caused by the hair's sharp, blunt-cut edge curling back and poking the skin of the follicle opening. This is worsened by sweat and friction from clothing.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">The Solution</h4>
              <ul class="text-sm list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
                <li><strong>Exfoliate twice weekly:</strong> Use a long-handle back scrubber. This keeps the follicle opening clear.</li>
                <li><strong>Avoid tight cotton:</strong> Wear loose-fitting, moisture-wicking shirts for 48 hours post-shave.</li>
                <li><strong>Moisturize:</strong> Use a lotion with salicylic acid (like CeraVe SA) to soften the skin and hair tips.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

