    <section id="pro-tips" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 opacity-5">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="200" height="200" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><path d="M12 16v-4"></path><path d="M12 8h.01"></path></svg>
          </div>
          <div class="relative z-10">
            <h3 class="text-2xl lg:text-3xl font-black mb-8">10 Pro Tips for a Perfect Back Shave</h3>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">1</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Trim first, then shave</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">If hair is longer than 1/4 inch, use a trimmer head or the Mangroomer's wide blade first. Then follow up with a foil or razor for smooth results. Skipping this step causes clogging and pulling.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">2</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Shave after a warm shower</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Warm water softens hair shafts and opens follicles, reducing the force needed to cut. This means less blade drag, fewer nicks, and less post-shave redness. Wait about 5 minutes after showering before shaving.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">3</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Use a two-mirror setup</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">One handheld, one wall-mounted at back height. This lets you see your entire back and spot missed patches. A bathroom door mirror angled toward a wall mirror gives a full view. Game-changer for first-timers.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">4</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Always go with the grain</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Shaving in the direction of hair growth significantly reduces irritation and ingrown hairs. On most men's backs, hair grows downward in the upper back and radiates outward from the spine in the lower back. Learn your grain pattern before your first session.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">5</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Moisturize immediately after</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Apply fragrance-free moisturizer or aloe vera gel to soothe skin and prevent razor bumps. Avoid products with alcohol (they sting and dry skin further). Cetaphil, CeraVe, or pure aloe work best. Apply within 2 minutes of shaving.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">6</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Clean blades after every use</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Rinse thoroughly under warm water after every session, then let air dry completely before storing. Never store a damp blade — moisture causes rust and bacteria buildup. For electric shavers, use the included brush to clear the foil.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">7</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Exfoliate 24 hours before shaving</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Use a back scrubber or exfoliating body wash the day before you shave. This removes dead skin that clogs blades and raises hair from the skin surface, allowing for a cleaner cut. Don't exfoliate immediately before — freshly scrubbed skin is more sensitive.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">8</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Use light, overlapping strokes</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Back shaving isn't the same as face shaving. Use consistent 6-8 inch strokes with about 20-30% overlap between each pass. Don't press hard — let the blade weight do the work. Especially critical with the Bro Shaver to avoid nicks.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">9</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Change blades before they feel dull</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">A dull blade requires more pressure, which increases irritation and nicks. For DE blades (Bro Shaver), change after 4-5 sessions. For BAKblade cartridges, change when you notice drag instead of glide. For electric foils, clean after every 3 sessions.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black shrink-0">10</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold mb-1">Maintain a consistent schedule</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Back hair is much easier to shave when it's short. Most men find bi-weekly shaving prevents the hair from reaching a length that requires a full trim-first routine. Consistency also reduces irritation — your skin adapts to regular shaving over time.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ========== FAQ ========== -->
    <section id="faq" class="mt-24 scroll-mt-24">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-10 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden" open>
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Does shaving your back make hair grow back thicker?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              No. This is one of the most persistent grooming myths. Shaving creates a blunt tip on the hair, which <em>feels</em> coarser as it emerges, but the actual diameter, color, and growth rate of the follicle remain completely unchanged. Dermatologists have debunked this repeatedly.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Is electric or manual better for back shaving?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              It depends on your priorities. Manual shavers (like the BAKblade 2.0 ELITE PLUS) offer the closest shave but have a slightly higher risk of nicks. Electric shavers (like the Mangroomer) are faster, safer for sensitive skin, and easier for beginners. If you have moles or back acne, go electric.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Can I shave my own back without help?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Yes. Every shaver on this list is specifically designed for solo use. They feature extended handles (18-20 inches) that reach your entire back, including the lower and mid-back. The Mangroomer's 135-degree pivot and the BAKblade's S-shaped handle are engineered so you never need to ask anyone for help.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              How often should I shave my back?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Most men maintain their back every 1-2 weeks. If you use a foil shaver like the Mangroomer, you can touch up every 4-5 days for consistently smooth results. It depends on your hair growth rate and how smooth you want to stay.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Do back shavers cause ingrown hairs?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Back shavers are less likely to cause ingrown hairs than waxing or epilating because they cut at the surface rather than pulling hair from the root. To minimize risk: shave with the grain, exfoliate weekly with a body scrub, and apply moisturizer after each session. The BAKblade's DryGlide technology is especially gentle on skin.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              What is the cheapest way to remove back hair?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              The Bro Shaver 3.0 at $13.99 is the most affordable option. It uses standard double-edge safety blades that cost about $0.10 each (you can get 50 blades for around $5), making it the cheapest to own long-term. No proprietary cartridges, no subscriptions. Over 2 years, expect to spend roughly $18-24 total including replacement blades.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Can I use a back shaver on other body parts?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Yes, most back shavers work on other large body areas. The BAKblade 2.0 ELITE PLUS blade cartridge detaches from the handle entirely, making it easy to use on your chest, shoulders, and upper arms. The Philips Norelco Bodygroom 5000 includes body guard combs specifically designed for trimming body hair across multiple zones. The Bro Shaver and Mangroomer are primarily designed for the back but can work on other areas with similar technique.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Is back shaving safe if I have moles?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              For men with back moles, electric shavers are strongly recommended over manual ones. The Philips Norelco Bodygroom 5000's individually rounded blade tips literally cannot cut into raised skin — they ride over moles and acne without catching. The Mangroomer's hypoallergenic foil is also safer than exposed blade systems. Avoid manual shavers (Bro Shaver, BAKblade) directly over raised moles — the exposed blade can nick or cut them, which can be dangerous and painful. When in doubt, shave around known moles rather than directly over them.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              How do I prevent razor burn on my back?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Razor burn is caused by friction from a dull blade, shaving against the grain, or shaving dry skin. To prevent it: (1) always shave after a warm shower, (2) shave with the grain of the hair, (3) use light pressure — never press hard, (4) apply a fragrance-free aftershave balm or pure aloe vera immediately after, (5) change blades frequently. If you have chronic razor burn, switch to the Philips Norelco Bodygroom 5000 — its rounded-tip hypoallergenic foil is specifically engineered to eliminate razor burn even on the most reactive skin types.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Is back shaving or back waxing better?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Both have tradeoffs. Waxing removes hair from the root, so results last 3-6 weeks before regrowth. However, it requires a professional visit ($30-80 per session) or an assistant at home, causes more pain, and carries a higher risk of ingrown hairs. Back shaving can be done solo, costs pennies per session after the initial purchase, causes no pain when done correctly, and results last 1-2 weeks. For most men, shaving is the practical long-term solution. Waxing is worth it if you want the smoothest possible results and don't mind the cost and discomfort.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              How long do back shaver blades last?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Blade lifespan varies by product and usage: Bro Shaver DE blades last approximately 4-6 full sessions before dulling (and can be flipped once to extend life). BAKblade DryGlide cartridges last several shaving sessions — replace when you feel pulling instead of gliding. The Mangroomer's electric foil head should be cleaned every 3 sessions and the blade replaced every 12-18 months of regular use. The Philips Norelco's shaving element should be replaced roughly every 2 years. Always prioritize blade freshness — a dull blade is the number one cause of irritation and poor results.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              What's the difference between the Mangroomer Ultimate Pro and the standard Mangroomer?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              The Mangroomer Ultimate Pro is their flagship model with two interchangeable head attachments (wide blade trimmer + foil shaver), the Power Burst button for thick hair, a more advanced shock absorber flex neck, and the PRO XL Extension Lock Button for precise handle positioning. Standard Mangroomer models typically include only one head and lack the Power Burst feature. For most men, the Ultimate Pro is worth the price premium — the dual-head system alone makes it significantly more versatile.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Is it safe to shave a back with tattoos?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Yes, provided the tattoo is fully healed (typically 4-6 weeks). In fact, shaving or hair removal makes tattoos look sharper and more vibrant by removing the "fuzz" that dulls the colors. However, never shave over a new or peeling tattoo. If you have raised scarring from a tattoo, use an electric foil shaver like the Mangroomer or Philips Norelco to avoid nicks.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              How do I avoid shaving over back acne or "backne"?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              If you have active backne, reaching for a manual razor is a recipe for disaster. The blades will catch on the bumps, leading to bleeding and potential infection. Use the <strong>Philips Norelco Bodygroom 5000</strong>. Its rounded tips and foil protect the skin while cutting the hair. Use a benzoyl peroxide wash 24 hours <em>after</em> shaving to keep follicles clear.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Can I use the Mangroomer in the shower?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              No. The Mangroomer Ultimate Pro is "rinsable" but NOT waterproof. You can rinse the heads under a tap, but the body contains electronics that cannot be submerged or used under a running shower. If shower shaving is your priority, choose the <strong>Philips Norelco Bodygroom 5000</strong> or the <strong>Hatteker 4-in-1</strong>, both of which are IPX7 rated.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              What's the difference between the BAKblade 2.0 and 3.0 Fuel?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              The 3.0 Fuel features a more rigid telescopic handle that reduces flex significantly, a better "Fuel" grip that doesn't slip when wet, and a built-in exfoliating brush attachment. While the 2.0 is still an excellent shaver, the 3.0 is a meaningful upgrade for guys who felt the 2.0 handle was a bit flimsy at full extension.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Does back hair grow back faster if you shave?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              No. Shaving has Zero effect on the actual follicle under the skin. It only appears to grow faster because you are cutting the hair at its thickest point (the base), so it becomes visible again quickly. Expect to touch up your back every 7-10 days for a smooth look.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

